<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have extended their applications from natural language tasks to practical workflows that require long-horizon reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patil et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trivedi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barres et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. Meeting this demand requires agents not only to master tool interfaces but also to accumulate transferable interaction experience from dynamic environments. Building diverse, stateful environments that faithfully capture real-world interaction logic has therefore become the foundation for scaling agent capability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies to scale agent training environments can be roughly categorized into two groups. One group, exemplified by Simia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and GenEnv <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, treats LLMs as implicit textual world models that generate feedback from tool specifications and interaction histories. These approaches substantially reduce environmental engineering costs, but the feedback generated can be hallucinated. Instead, a complementary family explicitly reproduces real interaction logic, synthesizing executable tools and environment databases: it extracts databases from real-world service themes, generates executable tools, and synthesizes tasks with verifiable results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>. As a result, these executable environments give agents a basis for learning interaction patterns that transfer across environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although environment scaling improves interaction diversity and out-of-distribution generalization, existing agent-training paradigms still rely on an idealized assumption: agents interact with stable, well-controlled environments under minimal user intervention. This mismatch with real-world deployment reveals two inherent challenges (as shown in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Lack of noisy feedback.</span> Real-world tool services are unreliable, frequently returning timeouts and service errors. Existing training environments usually assume stable and noise-free feedback, leaving agents underprepared for failure detection and recovery <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta, 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Lack of user-guided correction.</span> Real users actively intervene when an agent deviates from their intent, correcting its reasoning and actions. Existing user simulators often rely on standardized templates, with limited coverage of corrective instructions and diverse interaction styles during execution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barres et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suhr and Artzi, 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these challenges reveal that agents still lack proactive reflection under noisy external feedback. Ideally, a robust agent should continuously perceive environmental noise, task failures, and user correction intent, then promptly revise its actions. Existing reflection methods explore inference-time reflection, correction-trajectory learning, and noise-augmented training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bi et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, but most are trained in stateless or simplified environments, leaving one question open: <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">How can we elicit proactive reflective capability in agents from execution failures in real-world environments?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To address this gap, we propose <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span>, a general training framework for incentivizing proactive reflective thinking in agents. It models reflection as a learnable meta-skill: transferable meta-skills are explicitly mined from execution failures and progressively internalized into the policy through verifiable reflection learning. Specifically, <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> comprises three components:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Environment Reflection Skill Discovery (RSD).</span> We anchor 557 high-usage real-world environment themes and automatically construct database-backed executable environments and tasks, injecting diverse tool-return failures to emulate noisy conditions. From a diverse LLM agent pool, we sample trajectories covering varied task types and response styles, and matched success/failure pairs are then mined into 1,170 reflection meta-skills across 557 environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Reflective Supervised Fine-Tuning (ReSFT).</span> Given the skill library, we design three auxiliary and verifiable reflection sampling strategies: <span id="S1.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Self-Reflection</span> (SR), <span id="S1.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Skill Recovery</span> (KR), and <span id="S1.p9.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">User-Centric Reflection</span> (UR), which strengthen reflection at different levels. Supervised fine-tuning then internalizes these explicit skills into the agent policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization (ORPO).</span> To further elicit proactive reflection, ORPO combines Noisy Agent RL with reflection-guided on-policy distillation. For each task we match its environment’s reflection meta-skill and rollout in the noisy environment, optimizing the policy by reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards. The same action tokens are then re-scored under a privileged skill context, and a confidence-weighted OPD objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2026</a>)</cite> turns this contrast into dense supervision for reflection decisions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments on six long-horizon tool-use and noisy-interaction benchmarks demonstrate the effectiveness of <span id="S1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span>. Further analysis shows that it improves noise robustness and keeps training dynamics stable, supporting scalable general-agent training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, our contributions are threefold:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.25em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify two gaps between idealized synthetic environments and real-world
deployment in agent interaction, namely <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lack of noisy environment feedback</span> and <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lack of user-guided correction
during execution</span>, highlighting the importance of reflection learning for noisy interactions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RSD</span>, which systematically builds a scalable noisy-environment ecosystem by
incorporating real-tool perturbations and mining reflection meta-skills from large-scale success/
failure trajectory pairs sampled by an LLM Agent Pool.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further propose <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReSFT</span> and ORPO. <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReSFT</span> builds multi-level reflection data through
three sampling strategies and internalizes it by supervised fine-tuning; ORPO adds
reflection-guided on-policy distillation to Noisy Agent RL for token-level supervision of failed
executions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent Reflection Learning.</span> Agent reflection differs in where the corrective signal lives. Inference-time methods leave parameters untouched: Reflexion and Self-Refine append a verbal self-critique, and ExpeL retrieves cross-task insights at test time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Because the critique comes from the policy that failed, a well-formed but wrong tool return is rarely contradicted. Training-time methods move the signal into a per-trajectory repair: Agent-R, ReflectRL, and LoongReflect learn from that repair <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bi et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and NoisyAgent exposes the policy to perturbed interaction under only a scalar return <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Either way, nothing accumulates into an environment-level statement a later episode can reuse. <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> mines cross-model success/failure contrasts under the same injected noise into environment-indexed meta-skills and internalizes them through two-stage training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalable Environment Synthesis for Agent Training.</span> Diverse, stateful environments underpin tool use and long-horizon decision making. One line of work simulates interaction feedback with language models, treating tool specifications and dialogue history as an implicit world model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. A second makes the environment itself executable: EnvScaler and ScaleEnv synthesize tools with verifiable tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, Agent-World scales database-backed services over hundreds of real-world themes and verifies outcomes through state transitions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>, and EnvACE internalizes environment dynamics through world rehearsal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. All of them scale the <em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">supply</em> of solvable environments, whereas <span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> keeps the database-backed paradigm but turns the failures inside those environments into supervision, mining diagnosis and recovery rules from cross-model success/failure contrasts under perturbations derived from real tool errors. Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Supplement Related Work ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> gives an extended discussion of reflection learning, environment synthesis, and agentic RL.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology: Agent-Reflex</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview.</span> Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> summarizes the pipeline. To convert
environment failures into a reflection capability, <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> comprises three stages:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Environment Reflection Skill Discovery (RSD) (<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Stage 1: Environment Reflection Skill Discovery (RSD) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref">§ <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></span></a>)</span>. Build failure-aware executable environments and mine verifier-matched pairs into an offline skill library.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Reflective Supervised Fine-Tuning (ReSFT) (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Stage 2: Reflective Supervised Fine-Tuning (ReSFT) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref">§ <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></span></a>).</span> Convert each skill into verifier-validated self-reflection, recovery, and user-correction data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization (ORPO) (<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Stage 3: On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization (ORPO) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref">§ <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></span></a>).</span> Combine Noisy Agent RL with same-token skill-guided OPD to provide dense reflection supervision.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.17953v1/figure2_final.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/253;" width="476" height="253" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">RSD<span id="S3.F2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> and </span>ReSFT<span id="S3.F2.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.<span id="S3.F2.7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> </span>Stage 1<span id="S3.F2.7.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> perturbs tool returns of 557 MCP-anchored executable environments with mined failure families, contrasting success/failure rollouts into an offline skill library. </span>Stage 2<span id="S3.F2.7.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> samples local-repair (SR), global-recovery (KR), and user-correction (UR) continuations from that library, keeping only verifier-validated ones with skill prompts stripped.</span></span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Stage 1: Environment Reflection Skill Discovery (RSD)</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Noise-Grounded Environment Scaling</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To construct realistic environments with tool failures, we select 557 high-usage MCP server specifications from Smithery<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://smithery.ai/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://smithery.ai/</a></span></span></span>, each defining an environment topic <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Following Agent-World <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>, a research agent equipped with search, browser, coding, and operating-system tools builds and iteratively enriches a topic-grounded database. A coding agent then generates candidate tools and unit tests from each topic and its database. We retain only tools that compile, pass all tests, satisfy state-transition constraints, and support executable task verification. Thus, each environment <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a database, a validated tool set, and corresponding verifiers. The resulting ecosystem is <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}=\{\mathcal{E}_{m}:m\in\mathcal{M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}=\{\mathcal{E}_{m}:m\in\mathcal{M}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with 60 verifier-backed tasks per topic; the resulting task set <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> supplies the ReSFT data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tool-Failure Mining and Injection.</span>
Because real deployment is unstable, we ground perturbations in observed failures rather than invent them. We replay agents across nine public agentic benchmarks whose environments expose production-grade tool ecosystems and log every tool return (Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Stage 1: Environment Construction and Skill Discovery ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>). In total we collect 177,700 tool returns, of which 24,510 are error returns, and group them by surface form into eight actionable failure families (Table <a href="#A4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ D.2 Stage 1: Environment Construction and Skill Discovery ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). We then inject these failures into tool returns probabilistically to emulate real deployment:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t+1}^{\mathrm{train}}=\begin{cases}J(o_{t+1}),&amp;b_{t+1}^{\mathrm{inj}}=1,\\
o_{t+1},&amp;b_{t+1}^{\mathrm{inj}}=0,\end{cases}\qquad b_{t+1}^{\mathrm{inj}}\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p_{\mathrm{inj}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>train</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>inj</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>inj</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>inj</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Bernoulli</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>inj</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t+1}^{\mathrm{train}}=\begin{cases}J(o_{t+1}),&amp;b_{t+1}^{\mathrm{inj}}=1,\\
o_{t+1},&amp;b_{t+1}^{\mathrm{inj}}=0,\end{cases}\qquad b_{t+1}^{\mathrm{inj}}\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p_{\mathrm{inj}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the renderer <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> replaces the true return with a failure drawn from those compatible with the
invoked tool <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, balanced across the eight families so that rare ones still receive enough
instances for recovery to be learned. Here <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{inj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>inj</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{inj}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the per-return injection probability, and we keep clean and perturbed
tasks only if they admit a verifiable solution and recovery, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>Reflection Meta-Skill Extraction</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Capability-Diverse Trajectory Sampling and Pairing.</span>
Repeated sampling from one model can concentrate trajectories around its model-specific reasoning tendencies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We therefore sample trajectories from a capability-diverse LLM pool <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\mathrm{pool}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>pool</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\mathrm{pool}}</annotation></semantics></math> of nine models spanning five families, ranging from 4B to 284B parameters (Table <a href="#A4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ D.2 Stage 1: Environment Construction and Skill Discovery ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Each model interacts with the noisy ecosystem <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> on every task <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> under the same sampling protocol.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Because rule-based metrics cannot reliably assess long-horizon trajectories, an analyzer model and the environment verifier jointly label each rollout, yielding
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{correct}}=\{(q_{i},\tau_{i}^{+})\}_{i=1}^{N_{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>correct</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{correct}}=\{(q_{i},\tau_{i}^{+})\}_{i=1}^{N_{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{error}}=\{(q_{i},\tau_{i}^{-},e_{i},t_{i}^{\star},s_{t_{i}^{\star}})\}_{i=1}^{N_{-}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>error</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{error}}=\{(q_{i},\tau_{i}^{-},e_{i},t_{i}^{\star},s_{t_{i}^{\star}})\}_{i=1}^{N_{-}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> denote correct and erroneous trajectories, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the diagnosed error rationale, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is the first erroneous turn, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t_{i}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t_{i}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> is its environment-state snapshot. We pair correct and erroneous trajectories from the same task, limit the number of pairs contributed by each task, and require each environment to draw pairs from at least five distinct tasks, forming:
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pair}}=\{(q,\tau^{+},\tau^{-},e,t^{\star},s_{t^{\star}})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>pair</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pair}}=\{(q,\tau^{+},\tau^{-},e,t^{\star},s_{t^{\star}})\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Meta-Skill Extraction.</span>
An ideal reflective meta-skill is an environment recovery rule that maps an agent’s bad pattern to a corrective action, enabling reuse across tasks within the same environment. To mine such skills, an analyzer examines paired successful and failed trajectories, identifies recurring failure patterns, and converts them into executable recovery rules. For each environment <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, we store
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{m}=(d_{m},\mathcal{T}_{m},\mathcal{B}_{m},\mathcal{K}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{m}=(d_{m},\mathcal{T}_{m},\mathcal{B}_{m},\mathcal{K}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> describes the environment and its business objects, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> its tools, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> its observed bad patterns, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> the extracted reflection skills. The resulting offline library is
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}=\bigcup_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\mathcal{K}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}=\bigcup_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\mathcal{K}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>,
so each sample in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pair}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>pair</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{pair}}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a skill <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\in\mathcal{K}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\in\mathcal{K}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Representative environment-specific skills are shown in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3 Representative Environment-Specific Skills ‣ Appendix E Supplement Experimental Results ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Stage 2: Reflective Supervised Fine-Tuning (ReSFT)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ReSFT converts explicit reflection skills into policy behavior. Starting from a frozen behavior policy <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct three complementary forms of reflective supervision for local repair, global recovery, and user-guided correction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-Reflection Sampling (SR).</span>
For an erroneous trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> be its first erroneous turn, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> the environment-state snapshot taken before that turn is executed, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> the deployment-visible history up to that turn, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> the matched reflection skill. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\oplus z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\oplus z</annotation></semantics></math> denote appending <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> to context <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ver}_{s}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Ver</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ver}_{s}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> indicate whether continuation <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> successfully completes the task after restoring the environment to state <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. To teach failure awareness and self-reflection, we restore the environment to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> and continue after appending the corrective skill:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y^{\mathrm{SR}}\sim\pi_{0}(\cdot\mid h_{t^{\star}}\oplus\kappa),\qquad\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{SR}}=\{(h_{t^{\star}},y^{\mathrm{SR}}):\operatorname{Ver}_{s_{t^{\star}}}(h_{t^{\star}},y^{\mathrm{SR}})=1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>SR</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>SR</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>SR</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>Ver</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>SR</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\mathrm{SR}}\sim\pi_{0}(\cdot\mid h_{t^{\star}}\oplus\kappa),\qquad\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{SR}}=\{(h_{t^{\star}},y^{\mathrm{SR}}):\operatorname{Ver}_{s_{t^{\star}}}(h_{t^{\star}},y^{\mathrm{SR}})=1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The skill triggers diagnosis and replanning during generation but is removed from the stored input, so the policy learns to recover from its own error state.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Skill Recovery Sampling (KR).</span>
Many failures originate from an incorrect initial plan rather than a local tool error. To teach global recovery, we reset the environment to its initial state <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and restart from the original task with the matched skill:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y^{\mathrm{KR}}\sim\pi_{0}(\cdot\mid q\oplus\kappa),\qquad\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KR}}=\{(q,y^{\mathrm{KR}}):\operatorname{Ver}_{s_{0}}(q,y^{\mathrm{KR}})=1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>KR</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>KR</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>KR</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>Ver</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>KR</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\mathrm{KR}}\sim\pi_{0}(\cdot\mid q\oplus\kappa),\qquad\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KR}}=\{(q,y^{\mathrm{KR}}):\operatorname{Ver}_{s_{0}}(q,y^{\mathrm{KR}})=1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">As in SR, the privileged skill is removed before storage, encouraging recovery without an explicit skill prior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User-Centric Reflection Sampling (UR).</span>
When an agent deviates from the user’s intent, users may intervene and correct its reasoning. To teach user-centric reflection, a user simulator <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> converts the matched skill into a natural-language correction:
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\kappa}=U(\kappa,h_{t^{\star}},s_{t^{\star}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\kappa}=U(\kappa,h_{t^{\star}},s_{t^{\star}})</annotation></semantics></math>. We then restore the environment to <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math>, insert the correction after the erroneous turn, and continue:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y^{\mathrm{UR}}\sim\pi_{0}(\cdot\mid h_{t^{\star}}\oplus u_{\kappa}),\qquad\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{UR}}=\{(h_{t^{\star}}\oplus u_{\kappa},y^{\mathrm{UR}}):\operatorname{Ver}_{s_{t^{\star}}}(h_{t^{\star}}\oplus u_{\kappa},y^{\mathrm{UR}})=1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>UR</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>UR</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>UR</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>Ver</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>UR</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\mathrm{UR}}\sim\pi_{0}(\cdot\mid h_{t^{\star}}\oplus u_{\kappa}),\qquad\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{UR}}=\{(h_{t^{\star}}\oplus u_{\kappa},y^{\mathrm{UR}}):\operatorname{Ver}_{s_{t^{\star}}}(h_{t^{\star}}\oplus u_{\kappa},y^{\mathrm{UR}})=1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Because the user message is deployment-visible, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> remains in the stored input, whereas the privileged skill <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is removed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{correct}}^{\pi_{0}}\subseteq\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{correct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>correct</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>correct</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{correct}}^{\pi_{0}}\subseteq\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{correct}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote correct trajectories generated by the target policy <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. We merge them with the three reflective datasets:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{ReSFT}}=\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{correct}}^{\pi_{0}}\cup\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{SR}}\cup\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KR}}\cup\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{UR}},\qquad\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ReSFT}}=-\!\!\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{ReSFT}}}\log\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ReSFT</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>correct</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>SR</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>KR</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>UR</mi></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ReSFT</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mpadded style="width:0.632em;" width="0.632em"><mo lspace="0em">−</mo></mpadded><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ReSFT</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{ReSFT}}=\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{correct}}^{\pi_{0}}\cup\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{SR}}\cup\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KR}}\cup\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{UR}},\qquad\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ReSFT}}=-\!\!\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{ReSFT}}}\log\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Stage 3: On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization (ORPO)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ReSFT provides an offline initialization by internalizing explicit reflection skills. To further elicit proactive reflection from the current policy on its own on-policy rollouts, ORPO supplements outcome-based agentic reinforcement learning with token-level, reflection-guided on-policy distillation (OPD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, providing dense supervision for failure-causing decisions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.17953v1/Figure3_new.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/195;" width="476" height="195" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">ORPO.<span id="S3.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> A mixed rollout group of mostly clean and a few perturbed trajectories shares one group-normalized advantage. Re-scoring the same tokens with and without the skill card of their environment yields the detached, confidence-weighted OPD signal.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Noisy Agentic Reinforcement Learning.</span>
At update <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, we draw a task <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> from the EnvScaler RL task pool <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{RL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>RL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{RL}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, which ORPO directly reuses instead of <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RSD</span>-synthesized tasks, and construct one mixed on-policy group to expose
the behavior policy to noisy environment feedback. Specifically, the policy
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}</annotation></semantics></math> generates <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{q}=\{\tau_{q}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{q}=\{\tau_{q}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a fraction <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> rolled out in noisy environments
and the remainder in clean ones. All trajectories retain the complete tool interaction and final
answer, and none is given a reflection-skill prompt. After rollout, the environment verifier assigns
each trajectory a return <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{q,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{q,i}</annotation></semantics></math>; we normalize these returns within the mixed group:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{q}=\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}R_{q,i},\qquad\sigma_{q}^{2}=\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\bigl(R_{q,i}-\mu_{q}\bigr)^{2},\qquad\widehat{A}_{q,i}=\frac{R_{q,i}-\mu_{q}}{\sigma_{q}+\epsilon}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{q}=\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}R_{q,i},\qquad\sigma_{q}^{2}=\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\bigl(R_{q,i}-\mu_{q}\bigr)^{2},\qquad\widehat{A}_{q,i}=\frac{R_{q,i}-\mu_{q}}{\sigma_{q}+\epsilon},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where normalizing over the mixed group rather than within its clean and perturbed strata keeps
genuine failures inside the on-policy distribution without letting them form a contrast of their own.
For an agent action token <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{q,i,t,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{q,i,t,k}</annotation></semantics></math> at position <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of turn <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> under history <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{q,i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{q,i,t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we define the token log-probability of the current policy and its ratio to the behavior policy:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\theta}_{q,i,t,k}=\log\pi_{\theta}\bigl(a_{q,i,t,k}\mid h_{q,i,t},a_{q,i,t,&lt;k}\bigr),\qquad\rho_{q,i,t,k}(\theta)=\exp\bigl(\ell^{\theta}_{q,i,t,k}-\ell^{\mathrm{old}}_{q,i,t,k}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>old</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\theta}_{q,i,t,k}=\log\pi_{\theta}\bigl(a_{q,i,t,k}\mid h_{q,i,t},a_{q,i,t,&lt;k}\bigr),\qquad\rho_{q,i,t,k}(\theta)=\exp\bigl(\ell^{\theta}_{q,i,t,k}-\ell^{\mathrm{old}}_{q,i,t,k}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\mathrm{old}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>old</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\mathrm{old}}</annotation></semantics></math> the same score under <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Letting
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{q,i,t,k}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{q,i,t,k}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> mask the valid agent-generated tokens, which excludes tool returns and
simulated user messages, the clipped Agent RL objective is</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{RL}}(\theta)=-\,\mathbb{E}_{q,i,t,k}\Bigl[\chi_{q,i,t,k}\min\bigl(\rho_{q,i,t,k}\widehat{A}_{q,i},\ \operatorname{clip}(\rho_{q,i,t,k},1-\epsilon_{c},1+\epsilon_{c})\,\widehat{A}_{q,i}\bigr)\Bigr]\\
+\beta_{\mathrm{KL}}\,\mathbb{E}_{h\sim\mathcal{G}_{q}}\bigl[D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid h)\|\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot\mid h)\bigr)\bigr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>RL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{RL}}(\theta)=-\,\mathbb{E}_{q,i,t,k}\Bigl[\chi_{q,i,t,k}\min\bigl(\rho_{q,i,t,k}\widehat{A}_{q,i},\ \operatorname{clip}(\rho_{q,i,t,k},1-\epsilon_{c},1+\epsilon_{c})\,\widehat{A}_{q,i}\bigr)\Bigr]\\
+\beta_{\mathrm{KL}}\,\mathbb{E}_{h\sim\mathcal{G}_{q}}\bigl[D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid h)\|\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot\mid h)\bigr)\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon,\epsilon_{c},\beta_{\mathrm{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon,\epsilon_{c},\beta_{\mathrm{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> hyperparameters and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{q,i,t,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{q,i,t,k}</annotation></semantics></math> the mean over
all sampled token positions of the group. This component propagates only the outcome supervision of
the verifier; the reflection-specific token-level signal comes next.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reflection On-Policy Distillation.</span>
To turn the sparse outcome signal into dense supervision over reflective decisions, we condition on
the reflection meta-skills <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{q,i}\in\mathcal{K}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{q,i}\in\mathcal{K}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of the environment <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> that task <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> instantiates.
No retrieval or extra matching step is involved: each EnvScaler RL task already specifies its environment, and the skill library is organized by environment, so the
corresponding skill card is loaded directly with the environment and never depends on a benchmark answer. Prompting the same policy <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> with
this skill prepended to the task turns it into a teacher that re-scores the student rollout sampled
without any skill prior: a deterministic function <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> builds the skill-augmented history
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{h}_{q,i,t}=H(h_{q,i,t},\kappa_{q,i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{h}_{q,i,t}=H(h_{q,i,t},\kappa_{q,i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the current policy re-scores the already sampled
action token under it,</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\mathrm{skill}}_{q,i,t,k}=\log\pi_{\theta}\bigl(a_{q,i,t,k}\mid\widetilde{h}_{q,i,t},a_{q,i,t,&lt;k}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>skill</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\mathrm{skill}}_{q,i,t,k}=\log\pi_{\theta}\bigl(a_{q,i,t,k}\mid\widetilde{h}_{q,i,t},a_{q,i,t,&lt;k}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which is contrasted with the ordinary-context score <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\theta}_{q,i,t,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\theta}_{q,i,t,k}</annotation></semantics></math> of
Equation <a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.3 Stage 3: On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization (ORPO) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> on exactly the same token. OPD therefore needs a single environment
rollout stream: the skill branch only scores tokens as a training-time privileged teacher, and
gradients flow through the ordinary branch alone. Following SEED, the detached skill-induced shift
and its confidence weight are</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{q,i,t,k}=\operatorname{sg}\bigl[\ell^{\mathrm{skill}}_{q,i,t,k}-\ell^{\theta}_{q,i,t,k}\bigr],\qquad\gamma_{q,i,t,k}=\operatorname{sigmoid}\bigl(\beta_{\mathrm{opd}}\Delta_{q,i,t,k}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>skill</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sigmoid</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>opd</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{q,i,t,k}=\operatorname{sg}\bigl[\ell^{\mathrm{skill}}_{q,i,t,k}-\ell^{\theta}_{q,i,t,k}\bigr],\qquad\gamma_{q,i,t,k}=\operatorname{sigmoid}\bigl(\beta_{\mathrm{opd}}\Delta_{q,i,t,k}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">and we optimize</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{q,i,t,k}\Bigl[\chi_{q,i,t,k}\,\gamma_{q,i,t,k}\bigl(\operatorname{sg}[\ell^{\mathrm{skill}}_{q,i,t,k}]-\ell^{\theta}_{q,i,t,k}\bigr)\Bigr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>OPD</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>skill</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{q,i,t,k}\Bigl[\chi_{q,i,t,k}\,\gamma_{q,i,t,k}\bigl(\operatorname{sg}[\ell^{\mathrm{skill}}_{q,i,t,k}]-\ell^{\theta}_{q,i,t,k}\bigr)\Bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sg}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sg}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> blocks gradients through the teacher branch, so minimizing
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>OPD</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}</annotation></semantics></math> raises the student score on teacher-endorsed on-policy tokens. The
confidence weight <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a soft weighting rather than a hard filter: it emphasizes tokens
with a positive skill-induced shift and down-weights tokens whose shift is weak or negative.</p>
</div>
