<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setups</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span> We evaluate our method on two groups of benchmarks. (1) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Long-horizon tool use</span> covers general function calling and interactive workflows: BFCL-v4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patil et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, ACEBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, 200 stratified ToolSandbox tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barres et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and VitaBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. (2) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Noisy interaction</span> uses AgentNoiseBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>, instantiated on the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bench and VitaBench tool- and user-noise settings. Following the EnvScaler protocol <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, all evaluations use the standard MCP format and task templates, and each benchmark is scored with its official metric. Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Datasets ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> specifies every benchmark.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> Every trained method here, including <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span>, starts from the same Qwen3-8B backbone under one fair experimental setting. We compare three families: <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reflection methods</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">environment-scaling methods</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trajectory-level RL algorithms</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Baselines ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> specifies every baseline and Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> the implementation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Results on Long-Horizon Tool Use.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Stage 3: On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization (ORPO) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> performs well on five long-horizon agent benchmarks, achieving the highest overall score among strong baselines. We further make the following observations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Internalizing Reflection beyond Inference-Time Guidance.</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> outperforms Reflexion-8B and ExpeL-8B on VitaBench, ACEBench, and ToolSandbox. On VitaBench, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> achieves 17.5%, compared with 6.8% for Reflexion-8B. While these methods rely on self-generated critiques or retrieved experience at inference time, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> trains directly on verified recovery behavior. This may help the policy translate reflection into corrective actions more reliably, reducing its reliance on interpreting textual advice during execution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Reusable Recovery Knowledge beyond Individual Trajectories.</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> surpasses Agent-R-8B and NoisyAgent-8B on VitaBench, BFCL, ACEBench, and ToolSandbox. On ACEBench, it reaches 78.0%, compared with 52.0% for NoisyAgent-8B. These gains suggest that environment-level meta-skills provide useful recovery guidance beyond individual correction trajectories or episode-level rewards.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Reflective Supervision Complements Environment Scaling.</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> improves over EnvScaler-8B and Agent-World-8B on all five benchmark averages, e.g., 62.1% vs. 54.6% on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bench. On ACEBench, the gain comes mainly from multi-turn interaction: <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> reaches 73.3% at the turn level while matching the baselines at 85.0% step-level accuracy. This suggests that reflective supervision complements broader tool and task coverage.</p>
</div>
