<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Does On-Policy Reflective Optimization Improve Robustness?</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further assess ORPO’s effectiveness for reflection training, we analyze along two dimensions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Performance Comparison.</span> To evaluate online reflective learning, we train five representative algorithms and ORPO from Qwen3-8B for 100 RL steps on the same task pool. In Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2.2 Robustness under Interaction Noise. ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a), ORPO holds a stable advantage over GRPO, DAPO, and REINFORCE++ on BFCL V4, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bench, and real interaction noise. The advantage also holds against SEED, which already carries an OPD signal, and against noisy Agent RL, so reflection meta-skills summarized from real failures transfer better than experience estimated from rollouts alone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Training Dynamics.</span> To assess ORPO’s stability, Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2.2 Robustness under Interaction Noise. ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b) tracks mean rollout reward over the same 100 steps. ORPO separates from GRPO and SEED early and keeps rising after both flatten. We attribute this stability to the confidence weighting, which emphasizes tokens with a positive skill-induced shift and down-weights those with weak or negative shifts, so skill-neutral tokens do not dominate the update.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

