<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports an ablation study of
<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> on the two AgentNoiseBench settings. Each row removes
one component while keeping the remaining training and evaluation
conditions fixed, and the complete-model scores match those in
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2.1 Results on Long-Horizon Tool Use. ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We make three observations.
<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> Removing any component degrades both settings, showing
that the components are complementary.
<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> The supervision types follow a consistent ordering:
removing SR causes the largest drop, followed by KR and UR,
suggesting that failure awareness and local repair form the
foundation of reflective behavior.
<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3)</span> The skill library alone is insufficient: with the
library retained, removing OPD clearly hurts performance, while
removing only its confidence weighting also causes a decline.
Thus, the gains arise from the token-level reflection signal rather
than skill exposure alone.</p>
</div>
