<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Supplement Related Work</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Agent Reflection Learning</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agent reflection studies how interaction feedback can reveal errors and guide subsequent behavior. The methods differ along one axis that matters for our claims: <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">where the corrective signal lives</em>: in the context at inference time, in an offline trajectory rewrite, or in the parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference-time reflection.</span> The first family leaves parameters untouched and reflects in context. Reflexion appends a verbal self-critique to an episodic buffer that later attempts read <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>]</cite>, and Self-Refine iterates generation, self-feedback, and revision within a single episode <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>]</cite>; ExpeL instead accumulates cross-task natural-language insights and retrieves similar past trajectories at test time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>]</cite>. These methods bound what prompting alone recovers, and that bound is informative: because the critique is produced by the same policy that failed, it is only as good as that policy’s model of the environment. When a tool return is well-formed but wrong, there is nothing in the context to contradict the failed plan, so the critique tends to restate it. Our Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Stage 3: On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization (ORPO) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows this concretely: both Reflexion and ExpeL fall below their own backbone on the longest-horizon benchmark.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning from correction trajectories.</span> The second family moves correction into training. Agent-R combines MCTS with model-guided selection of the revision point to construct recovery trajectories, then learns timely correction by iterative self-training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2025</a>]</cite>. ReflectRL transfers reflection elicited on golden negative trajectories into direct reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bi et al., 2026</a>]</cite>, and LoongReflect combines privileged reflection supervision with outcome-based RL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>]</cite>, the closest prior work to our ORPO stage in supervision <em id="A1.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">type</em>, though its privileged context is an episode-level hindsight rewrite rather than an environment-level artifact. The shared limitation is that the correction is synthesized per trajectory: each repair is fitted to the episode that produced it, so nothing accumulates into a statement about the environment that a later, differently-failing episode can reuse.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robustness to perturbed interaction.</span> A third line attacks the problem from the environment side. NoisyAgent perturbs user instructions and tool responses, mixes clean and noisy rollouts, and adaptively raises noise difficulty, giving a complementary route to recovery and ambiguity handling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>]</cite>. This exposes the policy to failure states, which trajectory-level RL alone does not, but the supervision remains a scalar return: it can express that a perturbed episode was harder, not which recovery the specific failure admitted.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Positioning.</span> <span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> shares the goal of learning from failure and differs in what it learns <em id="A1.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">from</em> and what it stores. Before any policy training, it consolidates cross-model behavioral contrasts (matched success/failure pairs on the same task under the same injected noise) into explicit, environment-indexed reflection meta-skills. A skill is therefore reusable across episodes and mined once per environment, rather than rewritten per trajectory. These skills then supply supervision for autonomous recovery and user-directed correction (<span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReSFT</span>) and a privileged teacher context for token-level distillation (ORPO). Because the skill is privileged only during training and stripped from every stored input, inference needs no skill prompt and no auxiliary reflector, unlike the inference-time family above, where the critique must be produced online at deployment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Scalable Environment Synthesis for Agent Training</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing approaches split into two families. The first uses language models as implicit textual world models: Simia generates environment feedback conditioned on tool specifications and interaction histories, and GenEnv couples generative simulation with a capability-adapted curriculum <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>]</cite>. This removes most environment-engineering cost, but the fidelity of a simulated observation is bounded by the simulator’s ability to keep state consistent, so long rollouts can drift away from any realizable environment. The second family makes dynamics explicit through executable tools, databases, and programmatic verifiers. EnvScaler, AWM, and ScaleEnv construct environments whose transitions execute and whose outcomes are checkable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026b</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tu et al., 2026</a>]</cite>; Agent-World mines databases from real service themes, validates generated tools against them, and lets environments and policies co-evolve through continuous task generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2026a</a>]</cite>; EnvACE instead internalizes dynamics by <em id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">world rehearsal</em>, with one policy emitting both tool actions and the responses they induce <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2026</a>]</cite>; and TOUCAN takes a data-centric route, synthesizing tool-use trajectories from real MCP services <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These efforts are why a large pool of executable environments can be assumed today. What they do not address is the status of <em id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">failure</em> inside those environments: a verifier returning zero says an episode was hard, not which of dozens of tool calls broke the plan, what evidence exposed it, or which recovery the situation admitted. <span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> adopts the database-backed executable paradigm from this line and targets that residual.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Agentic Reinforcement Learning</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GRPO removes the value network by normalizing rewards within a sampled group <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>]</cite>, and DAPO and REINFORCE++ improve stability and sample efficiency through clip asymmetry, dynamic sampling, token-level loss aggregation, and variance reduction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>]</cite>. Applying these to tool-using agents introduces a difficulty single-turn reasoning lacks: a long episode’s return is one scalar attached to a decision sequence of unknown length. One line of work therefore reshapes the rollout or the credit assignment rather than the optimizer, through hierarchical rewards with a self-critic stage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2025b</a>]</cite>, entropy-triggered partial rollouts with soft advantage attribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2025c</a>]</cite>, and balanced exploration across the agent and environment branches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2025a</a>]</cite>. A complementary route leaves the reward untouched and densifies the gradient instead: on-policy distillation scores the student’s own sampled tokens under a teacher context, which SEED instantiates for agents by re-scoring the same action token with and without privileged information and gating a stop-gradient log-likelihood shift through a sigmoid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2026</a>]</cite>. ORPO is orthogonal to the first route and reuses SEED’s estimator unchanged in the second; what it contributes is the content of the privileged slot, an environment-level skill card mined once from empirical tool failures rather than a larger model or a per-episode hindsight rewrite.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Datasets</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All evaluations use the standard MCP interaction format and each benchmark’s official metric, reported by domain.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>General Function Calling</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BFCL-v4</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patil et al., 2025</a>]</cite> extends the Berkeley Function Calling Leaderboard from single-call accuracy toward agentic evaluation. We report Web, Memory, Multi-Turn, Non-Live, Live, and Irrelevance, and the average follows the official BFCL scoring procedure rather than a simple mean of the six categories. It is useful here because it separates call formatting from execution: a model can be near-saturated on Non-Live while failing the multi-turn categories.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ACEBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>]</cite> evaluates normal, special, and agentic tool use, emphasizing when to call a tool and how to handle underspecified requests. We report step-level and turn-level accuracy, and the average follows the official ACEBench scoring procedure rather than a simple mean of the two; the turn-level metric credits a turn only when every call in it is correct, making it sensitive to error propagation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ToolSandbox</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>]</cite> is stateful and conversational, with implicit tool state dependencies, an LLM user simulator, and deliberately unsolvable tasks that require asking rather than acting. We use a stratified 200-task subset and release the task identifiers so the subset is reproducible.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Long-Horizon Interactive Workflows</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barres et al., 2025</a>]</cite> is <em id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dual-control</em>: agent and simulated user both act on shared state. We report Retail, Airline, and Telecom and their macro-average. Each domain supplies a policy document the agent must respect, and success requires the final database state to match the target, so partially correct trajectories score zero.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VitaBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025</a>]</cite> covers delivery, in-store, and OTA domains plus composite <em id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross</em>-scenario tasks spanning domains within one episode. We report the four domains under official Avg@4 and take the benchmark average as the macro-average of the per-domain scores.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Noisy Interaction</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AgentNoiseBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026a</a>]</cite> injects perturbations into both sides of the interaction loop on the <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bench and VitaBench environments. Tool noise covers faulty, incomplete, inducing, redundant, and wrong returns; user noise covers ambiguity, topic shift, conflicting instructions, redundancy, and boundary probing. We score with the official Stability-Gated Success metric, which credits a task only when the whole trajectory stays valid and therefore excludes episodes derailed by noise that answer correctly by chance. The benchmark is out of distribution for us: our perturbations are summarized entirely from large-scale real tool-call failures, were designed without any reference to AgentNoiseBench, and are injected by a different process; no AgentNoiseBench environment is used in training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Training Task Pools</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two training pools must not be confused with the evaluation sets. <span id="A2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The <span id="A2.SS4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RSD</span> ecosystem</span> supplies the SFT data: 557 Smithery-anchored environments, each with a mined database, validated tools, executable verifiers, and 60 tasks (Section <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Stage 1: Environment Construction and Skill Discovery ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>), from which the <span id="A2.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReSFT</span> corpus is synthesized. <span id="A2.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EnvScaler tasks</span> supply the RL data: ORPO directly reuses 2.5K EnvScaler RL tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>]</cite> rather than <span id="A2.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RSD</span>-synthesized tasks, and every algorithm in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2.2 Robustness under Interaction Noise. ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> trains on this same pool, isolating the objective from the Stage 1–2 data.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Baselines</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Foundation-model rows are reference points at a different scale; bold and underline in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Stage 3: On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization (ORPO) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are computed among 7B–8B methods only. Most baseline results are taken from the numbers reported in the corresponding papers. Only for methods that are not open-sourced, or for benchmarks that a paper does not report, do we reproduce the method locally, following the settings and technical route of the original paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Foundation Models</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-5.2</span>, <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claude-4.5-Sonnet</span>, and <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini 3 Pro</span> are the proprietary reference points. <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-235B-A22B</span> is a large open-weight mixture-of-experts model included to separate scale from recipe. <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-8B</span> is the backbone for every trained method here, including <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span>, and is the control that matters: any margin over it is attributable to the training recipe rather than to capacity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Reflection Methods</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A3.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reflexion</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>]</cite> stores verbal self-reflection in an episodic buffer consulted on later attempts. It does not update parameters, so it bounds what inference-time reflection recovers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ExpeL</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>]</cite> accumulates cross-task experience as natural-language insights and retrieves similar past trajectories at test time: parameter-free, but with cross-task rather than within-episode memory.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent-R</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2025</a>]</cite> builds recovery trajectories by combining MCTS with model-guided selection of the revision point and learns timely correction by iterative self-training. It releases neither an 8B checkpoint nor its training data and does not report our benchmarks, so the rows marked <sup id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup> are our local reproduction following the original setting.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NoisyAgent</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>]</cite> trains on clean/perturbed mixtures with adaptively increasing noise difficulty. Since no checkpoint is released, its row in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Stage 3: On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization (ORPO) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (marked <sup id="A3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>) is our local reproduction following the original setting, and the optimization algorithm compared in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2.2 Robustness under Interaction Noise. ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is likewise reproduced from the description in the paper. Its row in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2.1 Results on Long-Horizon Tool Use. ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is quoted directly from the paper, whose reported <math id="A3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bench and VitaBench numbers come from RL on the evaluated domains themselves.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Environment-Scaling Methods</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A3.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="A3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulator</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>]</cite> is the model-simulated-environment route and <span id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TOUCAN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>]</cite> the trajectory-synthesis route.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EnvScaler</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>]</cite> constructs executable environments with verifiable outcomes and is the strongest environment-scaling baseline in our tables; because the ORPO stage of <span id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> reuses its RL task pool, it is the reference for attributing our margin to reflective supervision rather than coverage.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AWM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026b</a>]</cite> learns an agent world model over transitions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EnvACE</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2026</a>]</cite> internalizes dynamics through world rehearsal.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent-World</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2026a</a>]</cite> synthesizes database-grounded environments of controllable difficulty over 2,000 environments.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Agent Policy Optimization Algorithms</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These are the comparands in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2.2 Robustness under Interaction Noise. ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, all trained from Qwen3-8B on the same 2.5K EnvScaler tasks under matched rollout, tool-call, and token budgets.</p>
<ul id="A3.I3" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1em;">
<li id="A3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GRPO</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>]</cite> normalizes verifier rewards within a rollout group and is the base of our own outcome term.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DAPO</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>]</cite> adds decoupled clipping, dynamic sampling, token-level loss, and overlong-reward shaping.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">REINFORCE++</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>]</cite> strengthens critic-free optimization with baseline-based variance reduction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NoisyAgent RL</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>]</cite> appears here in its optimization role, as clean/noisy rollout mixing with an adaptive noise schedule, reproduced from the paper.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I3.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SEED</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2026</a>]</cite> adds same-action on-policy distillation to outcome-based Agent RL and is the most important comparand for ORPO, since it shares the estimator and differs mainly in what supplies the privileged context.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
