<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Training Guideline</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our aim is to test whether reflection can be mined, supervised, and internalized as a meta-skill, not to maximize a leaderboard number. All stages follow the cold-start-then-RL paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2025b</a>]</cite> so RL does not begin from a policy that cannot complete a trajectory, and every stage and every trained method shares the <span id="A4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-8B</span> backbone, with supervised fine-tuning implemented in LLaMA-Factory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>]</cite> and reinforcement learning in VERL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2024</a>]</cite>. All RL baselines and ORPO use the same experimental settings and hyperparameters. Tokens belonging to tool returns and simulated user messages are excluded from the loss; only agent-generated tokens contribute gradient, which is the mask <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{q,i,t,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{q,i,t,k}</annotation></semantics></math> in Equations <a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.3 Stage 3: On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization (ORPO) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.3 Stage 3: On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization (ORPO) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>; the same exclusion applies to the <span id="A4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReSFT</span> loss of Equation <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.2 Stage 2: Reflective Supervised Fine-Tuning (ReSFT) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Stage 1: Environment Construction and Skill Discovery</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Environments.</span> Environment topics are 557 high-usage deployed MCP servers in the Smithery registry, ranked by the registry’s cumulative <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">useCount</span>. Following Agent-World <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2026a</a>]</cite>, a research agent with search, browser, code, and OS tools mines a topic-grounded database and expands it for <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=3</annotation></semantics></math> complexification rounds; a coding agent then generates candidate tools with unit tests, and a tool is retained only if it compiles, passes its tests, respects state-transition constraints, and admits an executable verifier. Each environment contributes 60 verifier-backed tasks. Both agents are GPT-OSS-120B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2025</a>]</cite>, matching the Agent-World setting so that environment construction is not advantaged by a stronger builder.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Failure mining and injection.</span> Running agents across the nine public benchmarks listed below yields 177,700 tool returns of which 24,510 are errors. An analyzer model groups them by surface form into the eight families of Table <a href="#A4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ D.2 Stage 1: Environment Construction and Skill Discovery ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>; the families are multi-label, so one return may land in several counts. During construction each tool-return position is perturbed independently with probability <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{inj}}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>inj</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{inj}}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> per Equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1.1 Noise-Grounded Environment Scaling ‣ 3.1 Stage 1: Environment Reflection Skill Discovery (RSD) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Both draws are balanced uniform: the family uniformly over the eight rather than in proportion to mined frequencies, and the realization uniformly among those compatible with the invoked tool. A clean task is kept only if it admits a verifiable solution, and a perturbed task only if it admits a verifiable recovery.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks replayed for failure mining.</span>

The error returns above come from replaying agents across nine public agentic benchmarks, chosen to span interface types and task horizons rather than to maximize count. Many of them interact through MCP servers or shared workspaces and evaluate agents in those settings, which brings them close to real deployment. The nine benchmarks are MCP-Atlas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bandi et al., 2026</a>]</cite>, <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sierra Research, 2026</a>]</cite>, AutomationBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shepard and Salimans, 2026</a>]</cite>, OfficeQA Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Opsahl-Ong et al., 2026</a>]</cite>, JobBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026b</a>]</cite>, Harvey LAB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harvey, 2026</a>]</cite>, GDPval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patwardhan et al., 2025</a>]</cite>, Workspace-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2026</a>]</cite>, and SkillsBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026a</a>]</cite>. Together they reach MCP servers, REST suites, retrieval backends, and file or OS toolchains. They serve <em id="A4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only</em> as a source of observed error returns for the taxonomy in Table <a href="#A4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ D.2 Stage 1: Environment Construction and Skill Discovery ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>; none is used for training or reported as a result.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sampling, pairing, and extraction.</span> Trajectories come from the nine-model pool of Table <a href="#A4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ D.2 Stage 1: Environment Construction and Skill Discovery ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> under an identical tool-call limit, response budget, and sampling protocol, so differences reflect behavior rather than budget. DeepSeek-V4-Flash serves as both critique and analyzer model; together with the environment verifier it assigns the correctness label, and disagreements are discarded. Pairs form only within the same task, with at least five distinct tasks per environment; the analyzer then compares each pair and emits the environment record <math id="A4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, from which 1,170 reflection meta-skills are summarized in total.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training-Trajectory Sources and Policy Initialization.</span> The diverse pool produces behavioral contrasts for skill mining only; it never supplies a training target. Every trajectory the policy is trained on comes from Qwen3-8B itself: <math id="A4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{correct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>correct</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{correct}}</annotation></semantics></math> keeps only Qwen3-8B’s verified successes, and all Stage-2 continuations are rolled out by the same backbone. A stronger model appears solely as the analyzer for error diagnosis and skill extraction, a task Qwen3-8B is not reliable enough to perform. At the level of the policy that generates trajectories we therefore stay at 8B throughout, which is what keeps the comparison against the 7B–8B baselines fair.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We release the ReSFT and ORPO training data, the complete reflection skill
library, the Agent-Reflex-8B checkpoint, and the training and evaluation code
at <a href="https://github.com/RUC-NLPIR/Agent-Reflex" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/RUC-NLPIR/Agent-Reflex</a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Skill record schema.</span> A skill is a structured record rather than free prose: a trigger over visible history with its supporting and contradicting evidence, a diagnosis naming the failure family, a recovery template stating when to retry, switch endpoint, ask the user, or abort, and reversibility and partial-effect flags. Provenance and replay-validation fields travel with each record so that a skill remains auditable.</p>
</div>
