<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Stage 2: Reflective Supervised Fine-Tuning</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The three sampling strategies of Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Stage 2: Reflective Supervised Fine-Tuning (ReSFT) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> share one actor for continuation rollouts, <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}=</annotation></semantics></math> Qwen3-8B, the same backbone that is being trained, and one user simulator for UR, <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=</annotation></semantics></math> GPT-4.1, following the <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bench setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barres et al., 2025</a>]</cite>. A sample enters the dataset only if the environment verifier accepts the resulting episode. The final corpus contains 54K trajectories, combining verified Qwen3-8B successes with reflective-correction samples spread over SR, KR, and UR. Two kinds of privileged information are kept out of every stored input. The simulator state snapshot is used to <em id="A4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">restore</em> the environment before a rollout resumes and never appears as tokens in the context, so the policy sees only what it would see at deployment. The skill text is removed from the stored input in SR and KR, and in UR it never enters the context at all, since the simulator rewrites it into a natural user message <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math>; those synthesized user messages are retained, because a user turn is visible at deployment whereas a skill prompt is not. The UR simulator is prompted to cover direct correction, partial hints, ambiguity, and deliberately incorrect advice, so compliance is not always the right response.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Stage 3: On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ORPO starts from the ReSFT checkpoint with the skill library and reference policy frozen. ORPO directly reuses 2.5K EnvScaler RL tasks rather than <span id="A4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RSD</span>-synthesized data, which keeps the comparison against EnvScaler and the other RL algorithms direct. For the OPD hyperparameters, we follow the standard settings of SEED <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2026</a>]</cite> to ensure a fair comparison. Each update samples a task group, rolls out <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=8</annotation></semantics></math> ordinary trajectories of which a fraction <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=0.25</annotation></semantics></math> runs in perturbed environments, normalizes advantages over the whole mixed group, and re-scores the recorded action tokens under the skill-augmented context. The skill branch is a forward pass only: it never queries the environment and its gradient is detached, so the marginal cost over plain Agent RL is a forward pass rather than a rollout. The confidence weight <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a soft token weight: it emphasizes tokens with a positive skill-induced shift and down-weights those whose shift is weak or negative, while every trajectory still contributes through the outcome term. We further conduct an ablation study on the perturbation fraction <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>. Across the tested values, the performance remains relatively stable on the evaluated benchmarks, with only minor fluctuations and no consistent advantage for alternative settings. We therefore empirically set <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=0.25</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Meta-skill selection and environment alignment.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The EnvScaler RL task pool is entirely distinct from the RSD task pool, but
the two are aligned at the environment level: the more than 100
environments used to construct EnvScaler’s RL tasks are covered by the 557
high-usage MCP environments collected in Stage 1. Each environment and
synthesized task is assigned a canonical environment identifier, allowing
direct lookup of the corresponding skill:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{q}=\mathrm{Lookup}(\mathrm{env\_id}(q),\mathcal{K})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Lookup</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>env</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>id</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{q}=\mathrm{Lookup}(\mathrm{env\_id}(q),\mathcal{K}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, skills mined from RSD trajectories are transferred from the
environment level to different RL tasks instantiated within the same
environment, without semantic retrieval or task-level overlap. This
directly tests the reusability and transferability of the proposed
environment-level skills. At inference time, the skill prompt is removed,
so the deployed policy does not require access to the skill library.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.5 </span>Evaluation Protocol and Contamination Control</h3>

<div id="A4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every benchmark is scored with its official metric under the standard MCP format and task templates, following the EnvScaler protocol <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>]</cite>. VitaBench uses official Avg@4 with the benchmark average recomputed from per-domain scores. AgentNoiseBench uses Stability-Gated Success, and <span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> is measured under exactly that protocol. Following EnvScaler and Agent-World <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2026a</a>]</cite>, every score we measure is the mean of three independent runs, and decoding settings, maximum turns, and tool-call limits follow each benchmark’s standard setting. Most baseline numbers are taken from the results reported in the corresponding papers; only for methods that are not open-sourced or benchmarks that a paper does not report do we reproduce locally, following the original settings and technical route.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Noise taxonomy and row protocol in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2.1 Results on Long-Horizon Tool Use. ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</span> <span id="A4.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tool Noise</span> aggregates fault, incomplete, induce, redundant and wrong returns; <span id="A4.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">User Noise</span> aggregates ambiguity, topic shift, conflict, redundancy and boundary probing. The <span id="A4.SS5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Clean</span> results of foundation models follow the original AgentNoiseBench report and may therefore differ slightly from Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Stage 3: On-Policy Reflective Policy Optimization (ORPO) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. <sup id="A4.SS5.p2.1.5" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>Reproduced locally because the paper releases no 8B checkpoint or training data. The NoisyAgent row is quoted from its paper, which reports only per-domain aggregates, so its two noise buckets are left blank.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Because agent training on synthesized environments is easy to contaminate, we state the controls explicitly. Splits are preserved at the <em id="A4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">environment-family</em> level rather than the task level, since tasks within one environment share a database and tool set; generated instructions and skill text are checked for near-duplicates against benchmark instructions and tool schemas; and evaluation reference answers never appear in any analyzer, teacher, or user-simulator prompt. We release the task identifiers of all training data and the tool-fault injector used during training.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Supplement Experimental Results</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reports the fine-grained noise attribution deferred from Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Does <span id="A5.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> Act Reflectively Under Noise?</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Task success alone cannot show whether an agent repaired for the right reason. We therefore split the AgentNoiseBench noise settings into finer diagnostic slices (seven tool-side and six user-side axes, which Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2.1 Results on Long-Horizon Tool Use. ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> aggregates into its tool- and user-noise buckets) and score each with the benchmark’s Stability-Gated Success (<math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SGA}=I_{\mathrm{traj}}\!\times\!I_{\mathrm{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SGA</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>traj</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.052em">×</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>task</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SGA}=I_{\mathrm{traj}}\!\times\!I_{\mathrm{task}}</annotation></semantics></math>), which credits a rollout only if its trajectory was never derailed by the injected artifact, discarding turns pulled off course that answer correctly by luck. Each axis is normalized by its own three-model mean so noise-family difficulty cannot dominate the shape, with 95% paired bootstrap intervals over 165 tasks per axis. As shown in Figure <a href="#A5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ E.1 Does Agent-Reflex Act Reflectively Under Noise? ‣ Appendix E Supplement Experimental Results ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <span id="A5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> leads on all thirteen axes, with the margin widest exactly where each part of the design is meant to act.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.17953v1/Agent-Reflex_noise_radar_vector.svg" id="A5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/231;" width="476" height="231"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F5.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="A5.F5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Per-axis Stability-Gated Success on AgentNoiseBench’s native taxonomy<span id="A5.F5.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, normalized by the three-model mean on each axis (10 = that average). </span>(a)<span id="A5.F5.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Seven tool-side axes. </span>(b)<span id="A5.F5.10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Six user-side. <sup id="A5.F5.10.3.1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup> marks a lead clearing a 95% paired-bootstrap interval against <em id="A5.F5.10.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">both</em> baselines, <sup id="A5.F5.10.3.3" class="ltx_sup">†</sup> the backbone alone.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tool side.</span> The two axes clearing the interval against <em id="A5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">both</em> baselines are misleading lure (13.9, the largest tool-side margin) and incomplete information (10.9), the families whose returns are well-formed, so no schema check fires and only the evidence inside the payload reveals the fault. This is what <span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RSD</span> contributes and environment scaling cannot: more clean environments add successful trajectories, never a record of which well-formed return silently lied. On the four hard-failure templates, where the status code already names the fault, gains over the backbone are uniform (12.3–13.5).</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User side.</span> The largest margin is cognitive overload (12.9), the only user-side axis also clearing the interval against the stronger EnvScaler-8B, and the one <span id="A5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReSFT</span>’s user-reflection supervision targets directly: when the instruction itself is the defect, the repair is to re-scope the request rather than re-plan the tools, a behavior no tool-side reward can teach. Pooled over both sides, <span id="A5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> is derailed on 28.5% of rollouts against 44.8% for EnvScaler-8B and 56.1% for the backbone. Because the metric gates on trajectory validity, none of this is reachable by recovering an answer after being derailed: the gains are repair, not tolerance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Why AgentNoiseBench Is Out of Distribution for <span id="A5.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span></h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AgentNoiseBench is never consulted while <span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RSD</span> is built: neither its taxonomy nor its injector informs our perturbations, and the two noise designs are constructed in entirely different ways. The noise used by <span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> comes entirely from summarizing large-scale real tool-call failures: the eight families of Table <a href="#A4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ D.2 Stage 1: Environment Construction and Skill Discovery ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> are counted from 24,510 error returns observed while replaying agents on nine public agentic benchmarks, and are injected into tool returns of our own synthesized environments during construction and training (Equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1.1 Noise-Grounded Environment Scaling ‣ 3.1 Stage 1: Environment Reflection Skill Discovery (RSD) ‣ 3 Methodology: Agent-Reflex ‣ Agent-Reflex:Teaching Language Agents to Act Reflectively" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). AgentNoiseBench instead derives its categories from its own empirical analysis of interaction noise and uses a separate injector that rewrites tool returns and user utterances of the <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bench and VitaBench environments at evaluation time, covering seven tool-side and six user-side diagnostic axes. The two therefore differ in their source of noise, in the environments they act on, and in the injection process, and no AgentNoiseBench environment, task, or injector is used anywhere in training. Performance here therefore measures whether a reflective policy transfers to unseen perturbations, not whether a seen noise distribution is recalled.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Representative Environment-Specific Skills</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To make the mined library concrete, we show five records from substantially different domains in <span id="A5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">environment_skills.jsonl</span>. Each capsule preserves the four fields used by <span id="A5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RSD</span>: environment, representative tools, bad pattern, and extracted skill.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.SS3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="610.3" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 610.3" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,610.3) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#1B4F72;" fill="#1B4F72" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.98 L 0 605.32 C 0 608.07 2.23 610.3 4.98 610.3 L 472.4 610.3 C 475.15 610.3 477.38 608.07 477.38 605.32 L 477.38 4.98 C 477.38 2.23 475.15 0 472.4 0 L 4.98 0 C 2.23 0 0 2.23 0 4.98 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#EAF3F8;" fill="#EAF3F8" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.83 4.98 L 0.83 589.24 L 476.55 589.24 L 476.55 4.98 C 476.55 2.69 474.69 0.83 472.4 0.83 L 4.98 0.83 C 2.69 0.83 0.83 2.69 0.83 4.98 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.84 594)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:24.4em;--ltx-fo-height:0.83em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.53)" width="337.63"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.SS3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:24.4em;">
<span id="A5.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:120%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">AKShare One MCP Server</span></span>
