<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our reflection skill library is mined offline from a fixed snapshot of a large collection of executable environments. Consequently, it does not automatically adapt to changes in tool interfaces, service behavior, or environment dynamics. Such changes may require periodic re-mining or lightweight maintenance to keep the library aligned with the current environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Online updating, however, does not necessarily yield broadly generalizable skills. When the available environments or tasks lack sufficient diversity, skills summarized online may remain specific to individual environments or local failure patterns. The broader environmental coverage used for offline skill discovery may therefore help explain the stronger generalization of <span id="A6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> relative to SEED, although isolating this factor would require further controlled experiments. This highlights the importance of balancing update frequency with sufficient environmental coverage for skill generalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Future work will investigate online skill summarization and incremental updating across larger and more diverse environment collections, allowing the library to incorporate new environments and emerging failure patterns while supporting cross-environment generalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Broader Impacts and Future Directions</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scope of the current recipe.</span> <span id="A7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agent-Reflex</span> is validated on 557 executable environments anchored in live MCP services, eight empirically mined tool-failure families, and six evaluation suites spanning long-horizon tool use and noisy interaction, so the recipe is exercised well beyond a single benchmark. Its three stages are also deliberately modular: the skill library, the reflective supervision, and the token-level objective can each be replaced without touching the others, which makes the extensions below incremental rather than redesigns.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Future directions.</span> Three extensions follow naturally. First, the failure taxonomy is textual, mined from what an agent <em id="A7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sees</em> when a service fails; extending it to temporal signatures such as latency, concurrency, and partial writes would widen the range of deployment faults a reflective policy can anticipate. Second, the library is mined once at 8B scale, and testing whether it transfers across capacities, and whether skills compose across environments rather than only within them, would turn it from a per-recipe artifact into a reusable resource. Third, the privileged slot in ORPO accepts any environment-level artifact, so targets richer than reflection rules, verified sub-plans or tool contracts for instance, can be distilled with exactly the same estimator.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Broader impacts.</span> An agent that recovers from failure is more useful and also more consequential when wrong, since it takes more actions in the world than one that halts. Our skill records therefore carry explicit reversibility and partial-effect fields, and recovery templates distinguish asking the user from acting unilaterally. The <span id="A7.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReSFT</span> user-reflection data deliberately includes incorrect advice so the policy learns evidence-sensitive replanning rather than obedience. All environments are synthesized from public service specifications and contain no real user data, and because training perturbations are mined from real error logs, we release failure counts and families rather than per-service identities.</p>
</div>
</section>
